# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the UI tests.
#
# Almost empty on purpose, and it stays that way: what keeps the leak checker
# usable in a GUI is not this file but lib/sanitizer.py, which classifies every
# record by the frame that actually asked for the memory and lets a leak a
# library allocated for itself through without failing a test.
#
# The reason the two are not the same thing: LSan blames a leak on the nearest
# frame that is not in a system library, so pango's font and shaping caches are
# reported against whichever of our functions first asked pango to lay text out.
# Of the 633 records a clean startup and exit used to produce, 289 named our
# code that way -- the largest group, 101 of them, against update_tab_width() in
# mootextview.cpp, which frees all three of the things it allocates. Suppressing
# by the name a record is blamed on would therefore suppress the next real leak
# in that same function, which is the opposite of what a suppression file is for.
#
# So an entry here names a library that retains by design, and only where the
# classifier cannot help: a library that allocates through a callback of ours
# is charged to our frame correctly, and is still not our leak.
#
# Syntax: one "leak:<substring of a frame>" per line.

# Fontconfig builds its pattern and charset caches on first use and keeps them
# for the life of the process by design.
leak:libfontconfig
